WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
After every 30 compressions, you will pause to give two gentle breaths.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.480
Because an infant's body is so small and delicate, we have to adjust our technique

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.260
when delivering breaths to ensure we are providing oxygen effectively without causing injury.

00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:22.620
Just like with adults and children, we start by opening the airway using a head tilt, chin lift.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.280
However, for an infant, you must be extremely gentle.

00:00:27.799 --> 00:00:31.340
You want to tilt the head back just enough to reach a neutral position.

00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:37.340
Avoid overextending the neck, as this can actually block their narrow airway instead of opening it.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.540
To form a seal and deliver each breath, you will create a seal

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.160
by covering both their nose and mouth with your mouth.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.040
Do not pinch the nose.

00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:49.160
Remember, little lungs need little breaths.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.459
You are not giving a full breath like you would for an adult.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.779
Instead, deliver a gentle puff of air that lasts about one second.

00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.599
Your instructor will give each of you a chance to practice this sequence.

00:01:01.139 --> 00:01:02.840
Find the neutral head position.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.139
Create a seal over the nose and mouth.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.099
Deliver two gentle puffs, watching for visible chest rise.

